  "texts": [
    "Why does salt melt ice even when it's below zero?",
    "Ice is water molecules locked in a rigid crystal.",
    "But at the surface, molecules constantly break free and refreeze.",
    "At zero degrees both happen equally fast, a perfect balance.",
    "Salt dissolves in the thin film of water on the ice.",
    "Its particles get in the way of water trying to rejoin.",
    "Molecules still leave the ice, but fewer come back.",
    "So salty water only freezes at a lower temperature.",
    "But melting needs heat, and it steals it from around.",
    "So salty slush can get colder than plain ice.",
    "That's how you can make ice cream without a freezer.",
    "Ice and salt in a bowl drop to nearly minus 20.",
    "Below minus 10, road salt barely helps anymore.",
    "Then road crews spread sand or stronger salts.",
    "Follow for more physics hiding in your kitchen!"
  ]
